2. Getting Started with Cloud Shell and gcloud:
What is Cloud Shell?
Cloud Shell is a browser-based command-line environment provided by Google Cloud.
Go to google cloud console → authorize with your gmail credentials
→ search for cloud shell from search bar and select → open terminal
once cloud shell is selected → execute the followings commands
***

mkdir demo
cd demo
echo "Hello Cloud Shell" > hello.txt
cat hello.txt
chmod +x hello.txt
ls -l

cd ..

File & Directory Management
mkdir test # Create directory
rmdir test # Delete empty directory
rm file.txt # Delete file
rm -r folder # Delete folder recursively
cp a.txt b.txt # Copy file
mv a.txt folder/ # Move or rename file

Viewing & Editing Files
cat file.txt # View file content
less file.txt # Scroll view
head file.txt # First 10 lines
tail file.txt # Last 10 lines
nano file.txt # Simple editor
vi file.txt # Advanced editor

System Information

whoami # Current user
uname -a # OS details
df -h # Disk usage
du -sh * # Folder sizes
free -h # Memory usage
uptime # System running time

Environment Variables & Shell
env # List environment variables
echo $HOME # Print variable
export MYVAR=123 # Set variable
history # Command history
clear # Clear terminal

Next step is exploring the gcloud commands
***
Basic gcloud Configuration

1. Check active account
gcloud auth list

2. Check active project
gcloud config list project

3. Get help
gcloud help
gcloud compute --help

4. List projects
gcloud projects list

Using Cloud Shell Editor
Cloud Shell also includes a code editor.
Open it using:
cloudshell edit a.txt

//# a file will be opened named a.txt on editor; you
add text to it; go back to terminal and check its
content using

cat a.txt
Note: You may execute various programming files/codes as well

For Example:
cloudshell edit test.py

From terminal you can execute the file as follows:
python3 test.py

Compute Engine (VMs) // Works only with Billing
1. List VM instances
gcloud compute instances list

2. Create a VM
gcloud compute instances create my-vm \
--zone=us-central1-a

3. Delete a VM

gcloud compute instances delete my-vm \
--zone=us-central1-a

🔹 Cloud Storage // Works only with billing
1. List buckets
gcloud storage buckets list

2. Create a bucket
gcloud storage buckets create gs://my-bucket-name

🔹 App Engine
3. Deploy app
gcloud app deploy

4. View app
gcloud app browse

🔹 IAM (Permissions)
List service accounts
gcloud iam service-accounts list
